From a37c297951db45e88ae6238f3d3f92dd77f40f6c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Boyd Milan <76000029+jersonboydmilan@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 18:33:02 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] [docs] Document qvector targets for custom operations in
 Python vs C++

Since #4173, a Python custom operation accepts its target qubits as
individual qubits, a whole qvector, or a mix of both, as long as the total
matches the operation's target count. The C++ frontend does not: a
multi-qubit custom operation must be given each target individually, and a
cudaq::qvector is accepted only as the single argument of a single-qubit
custom operation (applied to each qubit) or as the controls of a controlled
one. Per the discussion on #4961 this difference is intentional, but the
"User-Defined Custom Operations" page did not mention it.

Add a short Python/C++ note after the two-qubit custom operation example,
reusing the my_cnot / MyCNOT operations that example defines.

Addresses #4961.

Signed-off-by: Boyd Milan <76000029+jersonboydmilan@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 docs/sphinx/api/default_ops.rst | 31 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 31 insertions(+)

diff --git a/docs/sphinx/api/default_ops.rst b/docs/sphinx/api/default_ops.rst
index d3923eb..48e2997 100644
--- a/docs/sphinx/api/default_ops.rst
+++ b/docs/sphinx/api/default_ops.rst
@@ -696,6 +696,37 @@ operations, each operating on 2 qubits.
       :end-before: [End Docs]
 
 
+Whether a whole :code:`qvector` can supply the targets of a custom operation
+differs between Python and C++.
+
+.. tab:: Python
+
+    The target qubits of a custom operation can be passed individually, as a
+    whole :code:`qvector`, or as a combination of both. The total number of
+    qubits passed must equal the number of targets of the operation.
+
+    .. code-block:: python
+
+        qubits = cudaq.qvector(2)
+        my_cnot(qubits[0], qubits[1])
+        my_cnot(qubits)   # equivalent to the line above
+        my_cnot(*qubits)  # equivalent to the line above
+
+.. tab:: C++
+
+    The target qubits of a multi-qubit custom operation must be passed
+    individually. A :code:`cudaq::qvector` is accepted in two positions only:
+    as the single argument of a single-qubit custom operation, which applies
+    the operation to each qubit in the vector, and as the controls of a
+    controlled custom operation.
+
+    .. code-block:: cpp
+
+        cudaq::qvector q(2);
+        MyCNOT(q[0], q[1]);  // supported
+        // MyCNOT(q);        // not supported
+
+
 .. note::
 
   When a custom operation is used on hardware backends, it is synthesized to a
